---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const mktCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The 4Ps: the classic toolkit', note: 'Product (what), Price (how much), Place (where), Promotion (how known). Every marketing plan is a decision in each — and they must agree with each other.' },
    { math: '2 · The funnel: awareness → action', note: 'See, think, do, care. Each stage loses people; the craft is finding the leak and fixing THAT stage — rather than shouting louder at the top.' },
    { math: '3 · CAC and LTV: the economics', note: 'Customer Acquisition Cost vs Lifetime Value. LTV/CAC ≥ 3 is the usual health bar. Marketing is an investment only when the ratio works — growth without it is expensive noise.' },
    { math: '4 · Channels are rented, brands are owned', note: 'Paid ads stop when the money stops; search rankings change; platforms change rules. Brand — the stored trust and preference — is the only asset the customer carries in their head.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/marketing-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Marketing is <strong>matching value to the people who want it, profitably</strong> — not
    advertising noise. The 4Ps structure the decisions; the funnel locates the losses; the
    LTV/CAC ratio judges whether the machine can run at all. Good marketing is mostly choosing
    the right customers and speaking to their actual problem.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="toolkit, funnel, economics, and the one asset you own" widget="equationStep" config={mktCfg} />

  <h2>Modern practice, honestly framed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positioning beats shouting:</strong> a sharp message for a narrow segment outperforms vague messages at scale — the positioning lesson's STP, executed.</li>
    <li><strong>Content and search compound:</strong> useful content and search presence are assets that keep earning (this atlas is one). Paid acquisition is rent; content is building.</li>
    <li><strong>Retention is the multiplier:</strong> raising retention raises every downstream number (LTV compounds). Retention problems cannot be fixed by more acquisition.</li>
    <li><strong>Measurement discipline:</strong> attribution is hard (multi-touch journeys, privacy changes); incrementality tests (geo experiments, holdouts) are the honest standard. Marketing science exists because the defaults mislead.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kotler & Keller; Thaler & Sunstein (nudges apply); Hermann Simon, Confessions of the Pricing Man; CAC/LTV practice literature.</p>
</TopicPage>
